{# _clean_window_rate_probe.j2 — calibrate DWT against an independent clock
   before the clean window trusts it.

   WHY THIS EXISTS.  The in-window checks (HPX_CLEAN_STALLED_ITERS and
   HPX_CLEAN_PARTIAL_ITERS) both derive their expectation from DWT itself: the
   zero test needs no reference, but the partial test compares each iteration
   against a warm sample measured with the SAME counter, moments earlier, in
   the same fault window.  That makes it scale-invariant -- if a stall slows
   the warm reference and the counted iterations by the same factor k, then
   k*iter < k*warm/8 reduces to iter < warm/8 and nothing fires.  A UNIFORM
   slowdown is undetectable by construction, and that is precisely the case
   the attach wait hands over when it times out: DWT is still stalled, so the
   warm reference is taken in the stalled regime too.

   Replayed numerically against the pre-fix bug (233 affected iterations at the
   measured ~0.6% rate), the in-window counters alone report STALLED=0,
   PARTIAL=0, avg 65,617 -- inside the observed 65,351-65,538 stalled band,
   with the host verdict "clean".

   WHAT THIS DOES.  Times a fixed nsx_delay_us() interval with DWT and reports
   the cycle count.  nsx_delay_us() resolves to the AmbiqSuite BOOTROM cycle
   loop: it uses no DWT, no CoreSight register and no debug power domain, so it
   keeps its calibration exactly when DWT loses its.  The expected reading is
   known in closed form from SystemCoreClock, so the host needs no tolerance
   guessed from model behaviour -- unlike a clean-vs-profiled comparison, whose
   legitimate spread (per-layer instrumentation overhead) is the same order as
   the fault it would have to detect.

   WHAT IT DOES NOT DO.  It is an instantaneous check taken just before the
   window opens, so it answers "is DWT broken going in", not "did it break
   half-way through".  The zero and partial counters answer the second
   question.  Neither covers a slowdown that begins exactly at window open,
   ends exactly at window close, and is perfectly uniform throughout; that
   residual is stated in docs/guide/power.md rather than papered over.

   Rendered wherever the clean window is timed per-iteration by DWT -- every
   transport, not just the ones with an attach signal, since UART and USB CDC
   have no host-drain signal to wait on and this is all they get.  ~1 ms, and
   entirely outside the measured/gated region. #}
#ifndef HPX_CLEAN_DWT_RATE_PROBE_US
// Long enough that nsx_delay_us's own granularity is noise against it, short
// enough to be invisible next to boot.  The host compares the reading against
// SystemCoreClock * this / 1e6.
#define HPX_CLEAN_DWT_RATE_PROBE_US 1000U
#endif
        // Verify DWT is actually counting, and counting at the right rate,
        // against a clock that cannot share its fault (issue #121).  Both
        // in-window checks below are DWT-relative and cannot see a uniform
        // slowdown; this can.
        uint32_t clean_dwt_rate_t0 = DWT->CYCCNT;
        nsx_delay_us(HPX_CLEAN_DWT_RATE_PROBE_US);
        uint32_t clean_dwt_rate_cyc = (uint32_t)(DWT->CYCCNT - clean_dwt_rate_t0);
